WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.128 --> 00:00:17.816
The south we traveled, the hotter the temperature was and the wider the river became.

2
00:00:19.076 --> 00:00:22.878
At times, it was almost two kilometers from one shore to the other.

3
00:00:23.899 --> 00:00:27.141
At night, we lay on our raft and looked at the stars.

4
00:00:28.241 --> 00:00:31.583
Sometimes we saw a shooting star streaking across the sky.

5
00:00:34.444 --> 00:00:38.988
One morning, as we were tying up the raft on the riverbank, I saw an old canoe.

6
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.132
I paddled it up a little creek to look for berries.

7
00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:46.976
Suddenly I saw two men running toward the creek.

8
00:00:48.077 --> 00:00:50.399
When they saw me, they begged me to rescue them.

9
00:00:51.159 --> 00:00:53.341
Men and dogs were chasing them, they said.

10
00:00:54.743 --> 00:00:59.567
I let them get aboard, and I paddled away as fast as I could until we got back

11
00:00:59.627 --> 00:01:00.188
to the raft.

12
00:01:01.672 --> 00:01:05.794
Jim and I and our passengers traveled on the raft a little way down the river.

13
00:01:06.915 --> 00:01:12.358
Then we tied up the raft on shore under some cottonwood trees, where all of us were hidden

14
00:01:12.578 --> 00:01:13.019
and safe.

15
00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.601
One of the men looked about seventy years old.

16
00:01:18.602 --> 00:01:25.025
He wore a battered hat, ragged blue jeans stuffed into his boots, and an old blue coat with

17
00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:25.806
brass buttons.

18
00:01:26.846 --> 00:01:32.657
The other man was probably about thirty years old, but his clothes were just as shabby as the

19
00:01:32.737 --> 00:01:33.277
old man's.

20
00:01:34.398 --> 00:01:36.459
Both of them carried worn-out suitcases.

21
00:01:37.999 --> 00:01:43.041
After we had breakfast and began to talk, I found out the two men didn't really know each

22
00:01:43.101 --> 00:01:43.521
other well.

23
00:01:44.482 --> 00:01:47.483
They had met as they were running away from two different towns.

24
00:01:48.523 --> 00:01:52.185
The old man said that he had been giving speeches in a small town.

25
00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:59.388
He had charged the townspeople ten cents each to hear him warn against the evils of drinking alcohol.

26
00:02:00.384 --> 00:02:06.967
Then someone saw him sneaking a drink himself, and the angry townspeople realized he was a fraud.

27
00:02:07.908 --> 00:02:09.569
That's when they chased him out of town.

28
00:02:10.609 --> 00:02:15.952
The young man had been in another town selling a medicine that was supposed to clean people's teeth.

29
00:02:16.972 --> 00:02:20.594
When people used it, however, they found that it damaged their teeth.

30
00:02:21.655 --> 00:02:23.756
That's why he had been chased out of town.

31
00:02:25.628 --> 00:02:30.852
Jim and I listened in amazement as the two men described all the different kinds of work they

32
00:02:30.892 --> 00:02:31.272
had done.

33
00:02:32.453 --> 00:02:36.276
The young man had been an actor, a printer, and a singing teacher.

34
00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:42.661
The older man claimed that he had cured people of diseases just by placing his hands on their

35
00:02:42.701 --> 00:02:46.484
bodies. "I think we should go into business together!"

36
00:02:47.204 --> 00:02:49.546
the younger man said eagerly to the older one.

37
00:02:50.407 --> 00:02:52.048
But then his face became sad.

38
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.781
"I can't believe that I have sunk so low in the world."

39
00:02:56.882 --> 00:02:59.543
"Why, what's wrong with my company?"

40
00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:02.264
the older man said, sounding insulted.

41
00:03:02.284 --> 00:03:07.667
"Nothing—it's what I deserve now," the younger man replied.

42
00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:11.428
"But, at one time, my life was very different.

43
00:03:12.729 --> 00:03:15.550
I trust all of you, so I will tell you my secret.

44
00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:19.792
My father came from England—I am the Duke of Bridgewater!

45
00:03:20.652 --> 00:03:22.953
But my wealth and title were stolen from me."

46
00:03:24.394 --> 00:03:26.455
Jim and I felt very sorry for him.

47
00:03:27.336 --> 00:03:31.958
He told us that if we wanted to make him feel better, we should call him "Your Grace"

48
00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:33.439
or "Your Lordship."

49
00:03:34.479 --> 00:03:36.200
We should also serve him his meals.

50
00:03:37.241 --> 00:03:39.562
That was easy enough, so Jim and I did it.

51
00:03:40.843 --> 00:03:45.986
By dinnertime, I could see that the older man wasn't too happy with all the attention we were

52
00:03:46.026 --> 00:03:46.726
giving the duke.

53
00:03:47.606 --> 00:03:50.148
Finally, he said, "Can I trust you?"

54
00:03:51.208 --> 00:03:52.750
We all assured him that he could.

55
00:03:53.650 --> 00:04:02.238
And then he said, "You see before you the poor wandering, unhappy king of France."

56
00:04:03.579 --> 00:04:07.682
He began to cry, and Jim and I asked him how we could cheer him up.

57
00:04:09.004 --> 00:04:13.187
He said that we should bow when we spoke to him and call him "Your Majesty."

58
00:04:14.628 --> 00:04:15.930
Jim and I said that we would.

59
00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.492
The duke now looked annoyed.

60
00:04:19.604 --> 00:04:25.088
When the king noticed, he said, "It's not our fault that one of us was born a duke

61
00:04:25.589 --> 00:04:26.670
and the other a king.

62
00:04:27.891 --> 00:04:30.012
Let's shake hands and be friends."

63
00:04:30.673 --> 00:04:31.434
The duke agreed.

64
00:04:33.835 --> 00:04:35.897
The two men were curious about Jim and me.

65
00:04:36.998 --> 00:04:39.019
They asked if Jim was a runaway slave.

66
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.981
"Goodness, no!"

67
00:04:41.401 --> 00:04:41.742
I said.

68
00:04:41.762 --> 00:04:44.404
"Would we be going south if he was a runaway?"

69
00:04:45.404 --> 00:04:50.148
I made up a story, telling them that my father and brother had been killed in a steamboat

70
00:04:50.328 --> 00:04:53.230
accident and that Jim was now my slave.

71
00:04:55.072 --> 00:04:59.735
I said that many people didn't believe me and thought Jim must be running away from his owner.

72
00:05:00.716 --> 00:05:04.279
We traveled at night, I explained, so that we wouldn't have any trouble.

73
00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.922
The duke and the king seemed to accept my story.

74
00:05:09.563 --> 00:05:10.824
It rained hard that night.

75
00:05:12.136 --> 00:05:16.239
Jim and I gave the two men our beds in the hut on the raft while we slept

76
00:05:16.299 --> 00:05:20.002
out in the rain, but it was so warm that we didn't mind.

77
00:05:21.082 --> 00:05:26.226
In fact, we were treating the king and duke so well that they decided to keep traveling with

78
00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:31.370
us. When we stopped along the river, they planned to go into towns and earn money.

79
00:05:32.931 --> 00:05:36.473
The king began to teach the duke speeches from Shakespeare's plays.

80
00:05:37.494 --> 00:05:40.316
He said they could charge people money to see them acting.

81
00:05:41.388 --> 00:05:44.031
He even had some shabby costumes in his suitcase.

82
00:05:45.572 --> 00:05:49.716
I had already decided that the two men were liars and cheats, not royalty.

83
00:05:50.797 --> 00:05:54.961
But I wanted peace on the raft, so I didn't say anything, even to Jim.

84
00:05:56.182 --> 00:06:00.907
Living with Pap had taught me that when you are with difficult people, it is better to stay

85
00:06:00.967 --> 00:06:01.387
quiet.
